00:03:17.340 --> 00:03:19.640
So, first of all, the
inner development loop.

00:03:19.640 --> 00:03:21.750
What is that?

00:03:21.750 --> 00:03:23.570
So imagine that you're
tasked with building

00:03:23.570 --> 00:03:26.150
an application or a
microservice that needs

00:03:26.150 --> 00:03:28.075
to run on a Kubernetes cluster.

00:03:28.075 --> 00:03:29.450
One of the first
things you might

00:03:29.450 --> 00:03:32.510
want to do after figuring out
basic tasks like firing up

00:03:32.510 --> 00:03:35.450
your favorite editor, setting
a version control, and so on,

00:03:35.450 --> 00:03:37.430
is actually running
your application,

00:03:37.430 --> 00:03:40.250
so opening up a web browser,
hitting the endpoint assuming

00:03:40.250 --> 00:03:42.740
it's a web app, and then
verifying and making sure

00:03:42.740 --> 00:03:44.960
that everything
works as intended,

00:03:44.960 --> 00:03:47.400
and then iterating on your
code, so in other words,

00:03:47.400 --> 00:03:49.740
setting up that inner
development loop.

00:03:49.740 --> 00:03:51.670
Now, since your
application is going

00:03:51.670 --> 00:03:53.450
to be deployed to a
Kubernetes cluster,

00:03:53.450 --> 00:03:55.670
it's probably pretty important
that your development

00:03:55.670 --> 00:03:59.110
environment mirror production
as closely as possible.

00:03:59.110 --> 00:04:01.440
This means running
on an actual cluster.

00:04:01.440 --> 00:04:04.700
Now, this could be a local
cluster like Minikube

00:04:04.700 --> 00:04:07.103
or a cluster on
Docker for desktop,

00:04:07.103 --> 00:04:09.020
or if you have the
resources available to you,

00:04:09.020 --> 00:04:11.690
it could be on a remote
cluster on one of the cloud

00:04:11.690 --> 00:04:14.240
providers like GKE on GCP.

00:04:17.110 --> 00:04:17.899
Right.

00:04:17.899 --> 00:04:23.550
So if you've ever tried to
set up this process manually,

00:04:23.550 --> 00:04:25.470
you know it's not
such an easy task.

00:04:25.470 --> 00:04:28.190
You have to figure out how to
build your container image,

00:04:28.190 --> 00:04:30.750
tag it, push it to a
container repository,

00:04:30.750 --> 00:04:33.930
update your manifests, deploy
everything, and then repeat

00:04:33.930 --> 00:04:37.048
this every time you change
your code, all of this

00:04:37.048 --> 00:04:39.090
while you're trying to
focus on writing your code

00:04:39.090 --> 00:04:42.960
and getting your business
logic working properly.

00:04:42.960 --> 00:04:44.700
So we think the
IDE is a good place

00:04:44.700 --> 00:04:46.422
to try and tackle some
of these problems,

00:04:46.422 --> 00:04:48.630
because we all know that
context switching can really

00:04:48.630 --> 00:04:50.140
kill productivity.

00:04:50.140 --> 00:04:51.390
So our tooling will help here.

00:04:51.390 --> 00:04:53.880
We'll create new targets
for you in the IDE

00:04:53.880 --> 00:04:56.280
in the form of new commands
in the command palette

00:04:56.280 --> 00:04:59.465
if you're a VSCode user and new
run configurations if you're

00:04:59.465 --> 00:05:02.090
an IntelliJ user that will help
you, with a single click, setup

00:05:02.090 --> 00:05:04.770
this development workflow.

00:05:04.770 --> 00:05:08.100
So the tooling will take
care of building the image,

00:05:08.100 --> 00:05:12.133
pushing it out to a repository,
deploying your application.

00:05:12.133 --> 00:05:13.800
And the tooling will
sit there and watch

00:05:13.800 --> 00:05:16.260
for changes in your
source code, and then

00:05:16.260 --> 00:05:19.310
surface it live on a cluster
every time something changes

00:05:19.310 --> 00:05:22.200
so you can quickly
verify your code.

00:05:22.200 --> 00:05:25.380
And again, let me stress, this
can be any Kubernetes cluster,

00:05:25.380 --> 00:05:27.510
so it can be a local
development cluster

00:05:27.510 --> 00:05:29.857
or it can work on any
of the cloud providers.

00:05:29.857 --> 00:05:31.440
And when you're done
with development,

00:05:31.440 --> 00:05:33.270
the tooling will take
care of cleaning up

00:05:33.270 --> 00:05:34.680
the deployed artifacts for you.

00:05:34.680 --> 00:05:38.190
So much like the feel of a
local development server,

00:05:38.190 --> 00:05:39.660
but with the power
and the fidelity

00:05:39.660 --> 00:05:43.020
of running on a real
Kubernetes cluster.

00:05:43.020 --> 00:05:46.440
All right, next, debugging.

00:05:46.440 --> 00:05:48.420
So at some point,
inevitably, you're

00:05:48.420 --> 00:05:51.520
going to run into issues
with your application.

00:05:51.520 --> 00:05:53.520
So at this point, it's
probably really important

00:05:53.520 --> 00:05:55.103
that you be able to
set up a debugger,

00:05:55.103 --> 00:05:57.120
so this means setting
breakpoints, stepping

00:05:57.120 --> 00:06:00.780
through your code, viewing the
call stack, local variables,

00:06:00.780 --> 00:06:03.580
being able to run
expressions, and so on.

00:06:03.580 --> 00:06:05.330
But remember, our
application now

00:06:05.330 --> 00:06:08.370
is running inside of a container
and on a cluster somewhere,

00:06:08.370 --> 00:06:11.910
so setting this up manually
is not really that trivial.

00:06:11.910 --> 00:06:15.030
You have to set up the proper
language-specific debug

00:06:15.030 --> 00:06:17.610
configuration,
inject that properly,

00:06:17.610 --> 00:06:19.500
setting up the
environment variables,

00:06:19.500 --> 00:06:23.755
doing the port forwarding, and
attaching a remote debugger.

00:06:23.755 --> 00:06:24.880
Our tooling will help here.

00:06:24.880 --> 00:06:26.690
Again, with a
single click, you'll

00:06:26.690 --> 00:06:28.440
be able to set up a
debugger and attach it

00:06:28.440 --> 00:06:32.130
to your service or even
your set of microservices

00:06:32.130 --> 00:06:33.780
and debug your
application as easily

00:06:33.780 --> 00:06:36.650
as if it were running locally.

00:06:36.650 --> 00:06:37.290
Cool.

00:06:37.290 --> 00:06:39.900
So now you can set up
your development workflow

00:06:39.900 --> 00:06:41.790
and you can debug your code.

00:06:41.790 --> 00:06:44.460
One thing, at this point, that
might be interesting to do

00:06:44.460 --> 00:06:47.530
