and without even needing
to write a Docker file.

00:31:44.300 --> 00:31:47.080
So let's check on the
status of our deployment.

00:31:47.080 --> 00:31:50.380
It looks like it's already
complete, so that's good.

00:31:50.380 --> 00:31:53.960
Now let's jump over
to the QA environment.

00:31:53.960 --> 00:31:55.686
So here we are again.

00:31:55.686 --> 00:31:57.493
I'm going to choose
to add a book,

00:31:57.493 --> 00:31:59.410
and I'm going to use
this auto awesome feature

00:31:59.410 --> 00:32:01.120
that Ian talked about.

00:32:01.120 --> 00:32:05.632
Now let's choose the same
book as before and save it.

00:32:05.632 --> 00:32:07.590
And so hopefully, now
the bookshelf application

00:32:07.590 --> 00:32:12.860
is going to be calling out to
my image recognition service.

00:32:12.860 --> 00:32:17.810
But, of course, we got an error
because this is a live demo.

00:32:17.810 --> 00:32:18.910
Let's try one more time.

00:32:18.910 --> 00:32:22.415
IAN TALARICO: You forgot
to change test to default.

00:32:22.415 --> 00:32:23.870
ETAN SHAUL: Oh, right.

00:32:23.870 --> 00:32:24.800
Thank you.

00:32:24.800 --> 00:32:26.900
So remember that I had
that injection issue

00:32:26.900 --> 00:32:30.230
where I was injecting
the test stub version?

00:32:30.230 --> 00:32:32.480
I forgot to swap that out
because we live reloaded it.

00:32:32.480 --> 00:32:34.313
IAN TALARICO: That's
how you know it's live.

00:32:34.313 --> 00:32:36.980
ETAN SHAUL: So thank you,
Ian, for catching that.

00:32:36.980 --> 00:32:41.180
So let's open up the controller
really quick, and here we go.

00:32:41.180 --> 00:32:43.700
Let's swap it out to
the default service.

00:32:43.700 --> 00:32:44.720
Perfect.

00:32:44.720 --> 00:32:48.380
So, at this point, we should
still be using the QA profile,

00:32:48.380 --> 00:32:49.848
and let's redeploy again.

00:32:49.848 --> 00:32:51.140
So this should be pretty quick.

00:32:56.340 --> 00:32:58.740
So it's going to build my image.

00:32:58.740 --> 00:33:00.790
Looks like the build
was successful,

00:33:00.790 --> 00:33:02.830
and the deployment was complete.

00:33:02.830 --> 00:33:04.570
So now the spring
boot app is starting,

00:33:04.570 --> 00:33:06.237
and it should be done
in any second now.

00:33:06.237 --> 00:33:06.900
There we go.

00:33:06.900 --> 00:33:10.812
All right, let's test
that out one more time.

00:33:10.812 --> 00:33:11.520
Let's add a book.

00:33:18.380 --> 00:33:22.460
Select "Effective Java"
again, and save it.

00:33:22.460 --> 00:33:23.420
There we go.

00:33:23.420 --> 00:33:25.420
This looks like it's
taking a little longer now,

00:33:25.420 --> 00:33:28.920
so it's hopefully calling out to
the right API, and there we go.

00:33:28.920 --> 00:33:32.320
We've extracted all the
features from the book image,

00:33:32.320 --> 00:33:34.200
and we didn't have
to type anything.

00:33:34.200 --> 00:33:36.857
So I think we're happy with
our application right now.

00:33:36.857 --> 00:33:38.690
Everything looks good
in the QA environment,

00:33:38.690 --> 00:33:40.520
so I'm going to
hand it back to Ian

00:33:40.520 --> 00:33:43.118
to show you some more
cool features in VSCode.

00:33:43.118 --> 00:33:44.660
IAN TALARICO: So
that's a good thing.

00:33:44.660 --> 00:33:47.300
If you guys realize how
fast that second build was,

00:33:47.300 --> 00:33:48.390
that was JIB, right?

00:33:48.390 --> 00:33:50.970
So that was considerably
faster than the first time

00:33:50.970 --> 00:33:53.960
because JIB was able to rebuild
the image for you faster

00:33:53.960 --> 00:33:56.820
with the existing
pieces of a Docker file.

00:33:56.820 --> 00:33:59.600
ETAN SHAUL: It granularly
layers the image

00:33:59.600 --> 00:34:01.950
so that it only needs to
rebuild the image to change.

00:34:01.950 --> 00:34:03.830
So it splits up your
Java application

00:34:03.830 --> 00:34:07.370
into its dependencies,
resources, and your source

00:34:07.370 --> 00:34:09.844
files, and only the
layer of the change

00:34:09.844 --> 00:34:12.469
gets rebuilt. So obviously, that
leads to much faster container

00:34:12.469 --> 00:34:14.150
builds.

00:34:14.150 --> 00:34:17.795
IAN TALARICO: Can we go
back to demo one, please?

00:34:17.795 --> 00:34:18.670
Cool, just like that.

00:34:18.670 --> 00:34:20.830
All right, so Etan
and I have talked

00:34:20.830 --> 00:34:23.170
through a lot of the
developer story for everyone,

00:34:23.170 --> 00:34:25.570
and you've seen the developer
inner loop and debugging

00:34:25.570 --> 00:34:27.370
and how simple we can
make that for you.

00:34:27.370 --> 00:34:29.290
So we're going to touch
a little bit right now

00:34:29.290 --> 00:34:31.713
on what be providing for
the DevOps side of things,

00:34:31.713 --> 00:34:33.130
looking at your
Kubernetes cluster

00:34:33.130 --> 00:34:35.708
and working with some YAML.

00:34:35.708 --> 00:34:37.250
So we're going to
jump right into it.

00:34:37.250 --> 00:34:39.458
So here, we have this
contribution here on the bottom

00:34:39.458 --> 00:34:42.502
left, and we can see here we
have a couple of explorers.

00:34:42.502 --> 00:34:44.710
So the bottom one, we have
a Google Kubernetes engine

00:34:44.710 --> 00:34:45.659
explorer.

00:34:45.659 --> 00:34:47.469
And this is a pretty
simple explorer.

00:34:47.469 --> 00:34:48.770
It has my account.

00:34:48.770 --> 00:34:52.159
You can see here the Google
Cloud project I'm using

00:34:52.159 --> 00:34:54.159
as well as the clusters it has.

00:34:54.159 --> 00:34:55.989
The explorer itself
is pretty simple.

00:34:55.989 --> 00:34:58.930
It gives you some creation
time, some public endpoints,

00:34:58.930 --> 00:35:00.550
the status of it.

00:35:00.550 --> 00:35:02.500
But what the real
power here is is I

00:35:02.500 --> 00:35:04.540
can right click and
set as active cluster.

00:35:04.540 --> 00:35:07.120
And that bumps it up
to my cluster explorer,

00:35:07.120 --> 00:35:09.260
and that's this
explorer up here,

00:35:09.260 --> 00:35:11.320
and this is where we have
all the great features.

00:35:11.320 --> 00:35:12.695
So right now, you
can see here, I

00:35:12.695 --> 00:35:14.380
have the QA and my dev cluster.

00:35:14.380 --> 00:35:15.790
And I'm going to
go ahead and I'm

00:35:15.790 --> 00:35:17.883
going to expand my QA cluster.

00:35:17.883 --> 00:35:19.550
And we're going to
see some information.

00:35:19.550 --> 00:35:21.633
So I'm looking at the
default namespace right now.

00:35:21.633 --> 00:35:24.140
Everything we did, we worked
in our default namespace.

00:35:24.140 --> 00:35:26.950
And we can see we have our
deployments, pods, services,

00:35:26.950 --> 00:35:28.660
even some custom
resource definitions,

00:35:28.660 --> 00:35:30.763
and we can do this for
any namespace we have.

00:35:30.763 --> 00:35:32.680
So if we go ahead and
look at our deployments,

00:35:32.680 --> 00:35:34.480
you can see here, we have
our three deployments

00:35:34.480 --> 00:35:35.407
deployed right here--

00:35:35.407 --> 00:35:37.240
the bookshelf frontend
and worker as well as

00:35:37.240 --> 00:35:39.020
that image recognition service.

00:35:39.020 --> 00:35:41.440
So if we looked at my dev
cluster, for instance,

00:35:41.440 --> 00:35:44.080
we wouldn't see that image
recognition service, which

00:35:44.080 --> 00:35:47.043
is why my original test failed.

00:35:47.043 --> 00:35:49.460
So we can go ahead and dive
into this a little bit deeper.

00:35:49.460 --> 00:35:51.520
We can see here our
bookshelf frontend.

00:35:51.520 --> 00:35:53.800
It gives us the
desired replica counts.

00:35:53.800 --> 00:35:56.510
We can see here this little
green dot in the corner here.

00:35:56.510 --> 00:35:58.740
I don't know if it shows
up on that screen as well,

00:35:58.740 --> 00:36:00.740
but that's just kind of
the state of everything.

00:36:00.740 --> 00:36:02.157
If it's green,
everything is good.

00:36:02.157 --> 00:36:04.540
If there's some issues, it
will show a little yellow icon

00:36:04.540 --> 00:36:06.560
there, and you can
dive and look into it.

00:36:06.560 --> 00:36:08.410
And if there's something
that's going wrong,

00:36:08.410 --> 00:36:10.868
you can definitely go ahead
and take a look at your events.

00:36:10.868 --> 00:36:12.800
These are events from
the last 24 hours.

00:36:12.800 --> 00:36:14.805
A lot of the times, if
something's going wrong,

00:36:14.805 --> 00:36:16.180
you can go ahead
and look at that

00:36:16.180 --> 00:36:17.805
and you can see some
of the issues just

00:36:17.805 --> 00:36:19.690
like that from your IDE.

00:36:19.690 --> 00:36:22.040
So I'm going to show you
one cool feature here.

00:36:22.040 --> 00:36:25.420
So if I'm just playing around on
a test cluster or dev cluster--

00:36:25.420 --> 00:36:28.180
I definitely don't suggest
doing this in production,

00:36:28.180 --> 00:36:29.650
but I want a view
or edit my YAML.

00:36:29.650 --> 00:36:31.990
Say I want a couple
more replicas.

00:36:31.990 --> 00:36:34.240
I can just go right
here and I can pull up

00:36:34.240 --> 00:36:35.470
the YAML from the server.

00:36:35.470 --> 00:36:39.230
So this isn't my local YAML,
this is my server YAML.

00:36:39.230 --> 00:36:41.760
And I see here I've
got two replicas.

00:36:41.760 --> 00:36:43.010
I changed my mind.

00:36:43.010 --> 00:36:44.020
I want three.

00:36:44.020 --> 00:36:46.150
I'm going to save
it, and it asks me

00:36:46.150 --> 00:36:48.550
if I want to apply
this configuration.

00:36:48.550 --> 00:36:50.950
Again, not necessarily
best production practices,

00:36:50.950 --> 00:36:53.290
but something you can do
if you're playing around.

00:36:53.290 --> 00:36:55.840
You hit yes, and
just like that, we

00:36:55.840 --> 00:36:59.560
can go back to our
cluster explorer,

00:36:59.560 --> 00:37:01.360
and we've got three replicas.

00:37:01.360 --> 00:37:03.400
So we can easily
change and modify

00:37:03.400 --> 00:37:06.160
the state of our Kubernetes
clusters and applications

00:37:06.160 --> 00:37:08.200
just from the IDE.

00:37:08.200 --> 00:37:10.330
We can also look a little
bit more into our pods

00:37:10.330 --> 00:37:13.268
to get some more diagnostics
about the application we're

00:37:13.268 --> 00:37:13.810
working with.

00:37:13.810 --> 00:37:16.840
You can see here, now we
have three frontend workers

00:37:16.840 --> 00:37:18.430
because we just added one.

00:37:18.430 --> 00:37:21.580
We also have our bookshelf
and the image recognition.

00:37:21.580 --> 00:37:24.740
And from these, we can go
ahead and view and stream logs.

00:37:24.740 --> 00:37:27.520
So if I want to view
logs, I can see the logs

00:37:27.520 --> 00:37:29.580
that came from this pod.

00:37:29.580 --> 00:37:31.510
And we can see here, we
had a couple requests.

00:37:31.510 --> 00:37:33.610
This, there wasn't too
much traffic sent to it,

00:37:33.610 --> 00:37:35.950
but we have our
post and our get,

00:37:35.950 --> 00:37:38.068
and we can kind of see
what's happening there.

00:37:38.068 --> 00:37:40.360
And if we want to dive a
little bit deeper, we can see,

00:37:40.360 --> 00:37:41.780
here's our bookshelf
application,

00:37:41.780 --> 00:37:44.380
and I could even get a
terminal into that pod,

00:37:44.380 --> 00:37:45.580
into that container.

00:37:45.580 --> 00:37:49.320
And you can see here, I'm
just going to do a quick ls.

00:37:49.320 --> 00:37:51.070
And you can see here,
all these files here

00:37:51.070 --> 00:37:54.430
is basically just what I
had in my explorer here.

00:37:54.430 --> 00:37:57.310
And it enables me to be able
to dive a little bit deeper

00:37:57.310 --> 00:37:59.380
into what's happening
and be right where

00:37:59.380 --> 00:38:01.700
my application is running.

00:38:01.700 --> 00:38:04.150
So let me go back to
our explorer here.

00:38:04.150 --> 00:38:07.060
We can, of course, take a look
at our services, replica sets,

00:38:07.060 --> 00:38:09.460
config maps, but we're
able to easily see

00:38:09.460 --> 00:38:12.460
the state of our applications,
dive in, and change them

00:38:12.460 --> 00:38:14.320
a little bit if we want.

00:38:14.320 --> 00:38:17.813
So onto the set of features
we have for our YAML.

00:38:17.813 --> 00:38:19.730
So we know that YAML's
a little bit difficult.

00:38:19.730 --> 00:38:20.563
There's a lot of it.

00:38:20.563 --> 00:38:22.450
When you start using
Kubernetes, maybe you

00:38:22.450 --> 00:38:25.600
add it on Knative and
Istio and Kustomize,

00:38:25.600 --> 00:38:27.430
and now you just
have piles of YAML.

00:38:27.430 --> 00:38:29.263
And one of the things
you want to try and do

00:38:29.263 --> 00:38:31.730
is help make that experience
easier for the user.

00:38:31.730 --> 00:38:34.460
So right here, this
is our deployment.

00:38:34.460 --> 00:38:36.890
It's a bookshelf
worker deployment.

00:38:36.890 --> 00:38:39.760
You can see here, whenever I
want to see what's going on

00:38:39.760 --> 00:38:42.980
and see what one thing is, I
can never keep these in my head.

00:38:42.980 --> 00:38:45.567
So a spec-- what's
supposed to go on a spec?

00:38:45.567 --> 00:38:46.900
There's quite a few things here.

00:38:46.900 --> 00:38:49.330
So we, just on hover,
give you the ability

00:38:49.330 --> 00:38:52.490
to see all the definitions
and potential fields.

00:38:52.490 --> 00:38:54.130
And we can do this
for any of these,

00:38:54.130 --> 00:38:55.420
and they'll give me an idea.

00:38:55.420 --> 00:38:57.130
It should be a string.

00:38:57.130 --> 00:38:58.725
It gives me a short description.

00:38:58.725 --> 00:39:00.850
And on top of that, since
we know that information,

00:39:00.850 --> 00:39:02.300
we can give you some completion.

00:39:02.300 --> 00:39:05.970
So if I want to add
something, I can

00:39:05.970 --> 00:39:08.010
get a list of all the
possibilities here,

00:39:08.010 --> 00:39:10.230
as well as the
definitions for them.

00:39:10.230 --> 00:39:12.788
So I know what I'm adding
and how I'm adding it.

00:39:12.788 --> 00:39:14.580
And again, we can do
some even more smarts.

00:39:14.580 --> 00:39:16.712
We know that our
replica is probably

00:39:16.712 --> 00:39:17.670
going to be an integer.

00:39:17.670 --> 00:39:19.128
If we hover over
this, I'm guessing

00:39:19.128 --> 00:39:20.460
it'll tell me it's an integer.

00:39:20.460 --> 00:39:23.940
And say we go ahead and
change this to cloud code.

00:39:23.940 --> 00:39:26.097
Cloud code should not be
a valid set of replicas.

00:39:26.097 --> 00:39:27.930
And we can see here,
we're getting an error.

00:39:27.930 --> 00:39:29.680
It's incorrect type.

00:39:29.680 --> 00:39:31.920
And so we can do some
type checking to help

00:39:31.920 --> 00:39:33.263
you a little bit along the way.

00:39:33.263 --> 00:39:34.680
And of course,
VSCode will give us

00:39:34.680 --> 00:39:37.620
this nice red not happy path.

00:39:37.620 --> 00:39:39.210
We can change that back.

00:39:39.210 --> 00:39:41.550
And so one of my
absolute favorite things

00:39:41.550 --> 00:39:44.550
that we can do
here in our YAML is

00:39:44.550 --> 00:39:46.320
we have some insight
into what's happening

00:39:46.320 --> 00:39:47.615
at the server or local level.

00:39:47.615 --> 00:39:48.990
So you see here,
we have a config

00:39:48.990 --> 00:39:50.700
map defined at the bottom.

00:39:50.700 --> 00:39:53.850
And you see there's a
little hover above it that

00:39:53.850 --> 00:39:55.470
says created four days ago.

00:39:55.470 --> 00:39:57.870
So the first time this
was created on my cluster

00:39:57.870 --> 00:40:01.890
was four days ago, and I can
dive into this a little bit.

00:40:01.890 --> 00:40:02.972
So it's a config folder.

00:40:02.972 --> 00:40:04.430
It's named config
folder, and I can

00:40:04.430 --> 00:40:06.130
peek the definition of that.

00:40:06.130 --> 00:40:08.550
And what happens is I
pop up a window here.

00:40:08.550 --> 00:40:11.130
And you can see here, this
is the local definition

00:40:11.130 --> 00:40:12.700
of this on my machine.

00:40:12.700 --> 00:40:14.340
So I can see my
config map is here.

00:40:14.340 --> 00:40:15.600
The name is config folder.

00:40:15.600 --> 00:40:17.593
This is what we peeked
the definition of,

00:40:17.593 --> 00:40:20.010
and then I have a project ID
which is just my Google Cloud

00:40:20.010 --> 00:40:21.290
Project ID.

00:40:21.290 --> 00:40:23.040
And if I want to see
what's going on again

00:40:23.040 --> 00:40:26.410
with this at the server level,
I can go dive into that too.

00:40:26.410 --> 00:40:28.180
And so here's my
server version of it.

00:40:28.180 --> 00:40:30.168
This is what's on my
active cluster right now.

00:40:30.168 --> 00:40:31.210
And I can look into this.

00:40:31.210 --> 00:40:32.918
We can see Skaffold
adds some labels when

00:40:32.918 --> 00:40:34.170
it does it's deployments.

00:40:34.170 --> 00:40:36.730
We can see our last
applied configuration.

00:40:36.730 --> 00:40:38.328
And if we go back
to our local file,

00:40:38.328 --> 00:40:39.870
want to make some
changes, I can just

00:40:39.870 --> 00:40:41.370
go ahead and click
onto that, and it

00:40:41.370 --> 00:40:44.430
takes me right to my code
or to my YAML in the IDE.

00:40:44.430 --> 00:40:47.160
So I'm more easily able
to work with my YAML

00:40:47.160 --> 00:40:49.360
and navigate around it.

00:40:49.360 --> 00:40:51.690
So these are all of the
features we have here for you

00:40:51.690 --> 00:40:53.040
on the DevOps side of things.

00:40:53.040 --> 00:40:55.290
If you want to see a little
bit more about this later,

00:40:55.290 --> 00:40:56.790
we have another
talk coming up we'll

00:40:56.790 --> 00:40:58.740
tell you about that
does a much deeper dive

00:40:58.740 --> 00:41:00.150
into some of these features.

00:41:00.150 --> 00:41:03.100
ETAN SHAUL: Let's just quickly
recap what we just saw.

00:41:03.100 --> 00:41:05.350
So first, we worked on
the bookshelf application,

00:41:05.350 --> 00:41:09.300
which was a two service app,
had a frontend and a backend.

00:41:09.300 --> 00:41:13.560
And we saw how easy it was to
setup this local development

00:41:13.560 --> 00:41:15.480
workflow, this inner
development loop.

00:41:15.480 --> 00:41:19.770
With a single click, we were
able to set up this development

00:41:19.770 --> 00:41:21.630
workflow where the
extension would

00:41:21.630 --> 00:41:23.190
monitor for changes
in your source

00:41:23.190 --> 00:41:25.730
and then surface them
live on the cluster.

00:41:25.730 --> 00:41:28.290
And remember, the application
was running on GKE

00:41:28.290 --> 00:41:31.980
in that case, on
Ian's dev cluster.

00:41:31.980 --> 00:41:33.750
You also saw Ian,
with a single click,

00:41:33.750 --> 00:41:35.910
attach a debugger
to the application

00:41:35.910 --> 00:41:39.740
and step through it, and it
behaved as if the application

00:41:39.740 --> 00:41:40.700
were running locally.

00:41:40.700 --> 00:41:42.830
It was just as easy as that.

00:41:42.830 --> 00:41:44.210
We then switched
over to IntelliJ

00:41:44.210 --> 00:41:47.150
to build the image
recognition service, which

00:41:47.150 --> 00:41:48.320
happened to be in Java.

00:41:48.320 --> 00:41:50.153
We showed you much of
the same functionality

00:41:50.153 --> 00:41:52.262
for the inner development loop.

00:41:52.262 --> 00:41:54.720
Additionally, we showed you
debugging the application which

00:41:54.720 --> 00:41:56.940
was running in
Docker for desktop,

00:41:56.940 --> 00:41:59.392
and making changes
to a class file

00:41:59.392 --> 00:42:01.350
and then live reloading
them, hot-swapping them

00:42:01.350 --> 00:42:03.360
right into the
running application,

00:42:03.360 --> 00:42:06.780
drastically reducing the
iteration cycle time.

00:42:06.780 --> 00:42:08.940
Next we showed you some
ops types features,

00:42:08.940 --> 00:42:11.970
so digging into your cluster,
looking at your pods,

00:42:11.970 --> 00:42:15.660
seeing some events, streaming
logs from containers,

00:42:15.660 --> 00:42:19.060
viewing remote YAML,
that kind of thing.

00:42:19.060 --> 00:42:21.692
And lastly, we showed
you some functionality,

00:42:21.692 --> 00:42:23.400
some editing support
for dealing with all

00:42:23.400 --> 00:42:25.440
of your YAML configuration.

00:42:25.440 --> 00:42:27.540
So what's next?

00:42:27.540 --> 00:42:29.310
Well, first, we would
love for all of you

00:42:29.310 --> 00:42:32.580
to try out these
tools and file issues

00:42:32.580 --> 00:42:36.570
if you find them on GitHub
and make feature requests.

00:42:36.570 --> 00:42:39.930
And we plan to continue building
out our Kubernetes support,

00:42:39.930 --> 00:42:43.090
so more direct support for
tools like helm and kustomize

00:42:43.090 --> 00:42:46.200
in the plugins, and more support
for Google Cloud Platform

00:42:46.200 --> 00:42:50.810
products like integrations
with things like Cloud Build.

00:42:50.810 --> 00:42:52.610
At this point, you
might be wondering

00:42:52.610 --> 00:42:55.658
how to get your hands on these
tools, and Ian will tell you.

00:42:55.658 --> 00:42:56.450
IAN TALARICO: Cool.

00:42:56.450 --> 00:42:57.260
Thanks, Etan.

00:42:57.260 --> 00:42:59.490
So the good stuff,
how do we get to it?

00:42:59.490 --> 00:43:02.600
So we've got the Visual
Studio extension.

00:43:02.600 --> 00:43:04.100
Cloud Code for
Visual Studio Code

00:43:04.100 --> 00:43:05.930
is in the VSCode marketplace.

00:43:05.930 --> 00:43:07.640
You can go ahead
and download it.

00:43:07.640 --> 00:43:11.660
Cloud Code for IntelliJ is
in the JetBrains plugins

00:43:11.660 --> 00:43:12.950
repository.

00:43:12.950 --> 00:43:16.250
And we have our documentation,
cloud.google.com/code.

00:43:16.250 --> 00:43:18.740
That QR code will take
you straight to the docs.

00:43:18.740 --> 00:43:20.570
And from our docs,
you can get linked

00:43:20.570 --> 00:43:23.460
to our issue tracking, feature
requests, things like that.

00:43:23.460 --> 00:43:25.130
And I want to stress
what Etan said.

00:43:25.130 --> 00:43:28.100
If you have feature
requests, ideas, thoughts,

00:43:28.100 --> 00:43:30.360
please go ahead and
leave them for us.

00:43:30.360 --> 00:43:32.347
It's a new product, we're
still developing it,

00:43:32.347 --> 00:43:34.430
and we're building it for
you, for the developers,

00:43:34.430 --> 00:43:35.060
for the DevOps.

00:43:35.060 --> 00:43:36.893
So we want you to be
happy with the product.

00:43:36.893 --> 00:43:38.990
We want you to have
the features in it

00:43:38.990 --> 00:43:41.130
that you want to be
successful in your jobs.

00:43:41.130 --> 00:43:43.610
So we greatly accept feedback.

00:43:43.610 --> 00:43:45.620
So back to Etan.

00:43:45.620 --> 00:43:46.370
ETAN SHAUL: Great.

